N1  - Closest precedent found for InVEST SWY applied across genuinely different regions/landscapes in one study. Full text obtained and read 2026-07-17. Key findings: neither case study derived CN/Kc from literature tables from scratch -- Peru reused CN from an existing calibrated SWAT model of that exact basin (Uribe et al. 2013), Myanmar reused CN/Kc from an existing national ecosystem-service assessment (Mandle et al. 2017) -- the actual operational precedent for "how to build subregional tables" is to borrow from existing calibrated regional studies, not derive from TR-55 tables cold. CN sensitivity found to be dramatic: quickflow changed by factors of 10.1-13.1x between antecedent-moisture-condition settings in the Chindwin basin. Also found model reliability degrades at large basin scale -- their largest basin (114,000 km2) already showed spatial-variation problems, and their own conclusion recommends caution with absolute values in new geographies, with basins under 10,000 km2 best supported by their validation data. No true global-scale InVEST SWY application was found anywhere in this review -- worth treating "one global run" itself as an open question, not just the CN table.
