TI  - An Empirical Algorithm for Estimating Agricultural and Riparian Evapotranspiration Using MODIS Enhanced Vegetation Index and Ground Measurements of ET. I. Description of Method
JO  - Remote Sensing
VL  - 1
IS  - 4
SP  - 1273
EP  - 1297
DO  - 10.3390/rs1041273
UR  - https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/1/4/1273
N1  - Read in full 2026-09-22 (user obtained the PDF, now in docs/swy/literature_pdfs/nagler2009.pdf), confirming and sharpening the earlier WebSearch-summary version of this note. Equation confirmed exactly: EToF = 1.22 x EVI* (r2=0.55 for saltcedar alone, "low predictive power" -- the paper's own words -- improving somewhat pooled across plant types; ~20% error overall). Real, previously-missed caveat: EVI* is NOT raw EVI -- it's rescaled via EVI* = 1-(EVImax-EVI)/(EVImax-EVImin), using EVImax=0.542/EVImin=0.091 taken from a prior study of three specific western riparian zones. Those bounds are locally-derived, not universal -- reusing them for a totally different vegetation type (e.g. closed-canopy tropical forest, which would have a much higher real EVImax) without re-deriving EVImax/EVImin would repeat exactly the kind of unvalidated-substitution error already found in this project's own Kamble/EVI use. The paper's own authors explicitly warn against this: "the ecophysiological constraints on EG should be characterized by ground studies in a biome of interest, before remote sensing methods are used to scale physiological functions such as EG" -- a direct statement against blind transfer to a new biome, not an incidental caveat. Study vegetation: alfalfa (irrigated cropland), saltcedar, cottonwood, arrowweed -- desert riparian phreatophytes and irrigated cropland in an extreme low-desert environment (<100mm/yr rainfall), Lower Colorado River, AZ/CA. Solves the "wrong vegetation index" problem Kamble has, not the "wrong ecosystem" problem -- confirmed real fallback candidate for semi-arid/desert riparian and irrigated-cropland Kc specifically, not usable elsewhere without new EVImax/EVImin calibration. Full citation to Negron Juarez et al. 2008 appears in this paper's own reference list (ref. 9) -- same research lineage, already linked in the literature independently of this project's own search.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Valle Junior, Luiz Claudio Galvão do
AU  - Rodrigues, Dulce Buchala Bicca
AU  - Oliveira, Paulo Tarso Sanches de
PY  - 2019
TI  - Initial abstraction ratio and Curve Number estimation using rainfall and runoff data from a tropical watershed
JO  - Revista Brasileira de Recursos Hidricos
VL  - 24
SP  - e5
DO  - 10.1590/2318-0331.241920170199
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1590/2318-0331.241920170199
N1  - Read in full 2026-09-22 (open access, PDF in docs/swy/literature_pdfs/vallejunior2019.pdf). NOT a match for the "native vs. thinned dry tropical forest" description an earlier search summary suggested -- correcting that here. Real study: Guariroba basin, Midwestern Brazil (Campo Grande, MS), Koppen Am (tropical monsoon, explicitly stated by the paper itself), 73.27% pasture land use, predominantly sandy soil (hydrologic group A, high infiltration), 30 real rainfall-runoff events. Same Oliveira as oliveira2016 (Cerrado) -- same ongoing research program, not a coincidence. The actual, more important finding: the SCS-CN method itself was found NOT SUITABLE for this basin -- negative Nash-Sutcliffe Efficiency across every CN-fitting method tested (median, geometric/arithmetic mean, nonlinear least-squares, and asymptotic fit under most configurations), "complacent" behavior (CN keeps declining with storm size rather than approaching a stable value), attributed to runoff being dominated by subsurface stormflow rather than the Hortonian infiltration-excess mechanism CN assumes. A simple linear runoff coefficient model (Q=CP, C~0.025) fit far better (R2=0.71, NSE=0.66, PBIAS=-2.02, all "good" by the paper's own thresholds) than any CN variant. This parallels Liu et al. 2017's EBF finding (Kc not correlating with vegetation index at all) -- a second, independent case of "the standard model framework itself breaks down here," not just "needs different parameters." Relevant biome: land use is pasture-dominated, closer to Tropical & Subtropical Grasslands/Savannas/Shrublands than to dry broadleaf forest -- directly answers the open question already flagged elsewhere (whether Oliveira 2016/Cerrado-style findings extend beyond the flooded-grasslands variant). Also: median initial abstraction ratio lambda=0.045 vs. the NRCS standard 0.2, reinforcing the broader pattern that standard US-derived CN parameters don't transfer to tropical settings.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Negrón Juárez, Robinson I.
AU  - Goulden, Michael L.
AU  - Myneni, Ranga B.
AU  - Fu, Rong
AU  - Bernardes, Sergio
AU  - Gao, Huilin
PY  - 2008
TI  - An empirical approach to retrieving monthly evapotranspiration over Amazonia
JO  - International Journal of Remote Sensing
VL  - 29
IS  - 24
SP  - 7045
EP  - 7063
DO  - 10.1080/01431160802226026
UR  - https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01431160802226026
N1  - Read in full 2026-09-22 (user obtained the PDF via institutional access, docs/swy/literature_pdfs/negronjuarez2008.pdf). DOI confirmed correct via Crossref; "H. Gao" is real, the sixth of six co-authors, not a misattributed lead. This is the paper this project has been trying to obtain for months, and it's genuinely more valuable than Kamble or Nagler -- but structurally different from both, not a drop-in swap. The model is NOT a simple Kc=a*EVI+b regression: ET = C1 + C2*EVI^C3*(Rn-C4), EVI combined multiplicatively with net radiation (from ISCCP), not alone. Fitted general model (ET_grl): C1=2.7, C2=0.05, C3=1.75, C4=140. Trained/validated on 8 upland tropical rainforest flux-tower sites across the Brazilian Legal Amazon (K83, RJA for training; K67, K34 for validation; CRS, DCK, SIN, BRG for comparison) -- genuinely tropical rainforest, unlike Kamble (temperate cropland) or Nagler (desert riparian/irrigated cropland). R2 between modelled and observed ET: 0.61 (K83), 0.55 (K67), 0.8 (K34), but only 0.31 (RJA, where the model also overestimated the annual maximum) -- real, honestly mixed validation, not uniformly strong. Average error ~17%, up to ~30% at the weakest site. Real adoption costs, not a quick fix: (1) needs a net-radiation input layer (ISCCP or equivalent) not currently in this project's Philippines pipeline, in addition to EVI; (2) the fitted constants (especially C4, an Rn offset) are Amazon-specific -- the closest ecosystem analog found so far for tropical moist broadleaf forest, but still a different tropical region (Amazon vs. Philippines/SE Asia), and would need at least a plausibility check, likely re-fitting, before real use; (3) the paper's own conclusion explicitly flags that further study is needed before applying it to land-cover-converted areas, i.e. validated for intact upland forest specifically. Doesn't resolve the Liu et al. 2017 question (whether tropical forest Kc is fundamentally energy-limited rather than vegetation-index-limited) so much as sidestep it -- the multiplicative EVI-times-(Rn-C4) form already builds in an energy term rather than relying on vegetation index alone the way Kamble/Nagler do, consistent with that concern.
ER  -

