N1  - Added 2026-09-22, full text actually read (open access, Copernicus/EGU, no paywall) after a WebSearch summary made it sound like a bigger win than it turned out to be -- worth being precise about what it does and doesn't give us. Global synthesis of Kc (= AET/PET) from 81 FLUXNET eddy-flux sites across 7 IGBP land-cover types, with multiple linear regression models (Table 1) using LAI + site latitude + monthly precipitation as predictors, by land cover and season. Real limitation for this project's actual need: only 5 evergreen broadleaf forest (EBF) sites total, and the paper states most sites are 30-60N, "except three EBF sites" -- almost certainly not dominated by genuine equatorial tropical rainforest the way Negron Juarez (Amazonia-specific) would be. The EBF regression has NO significant LAI/latitude/precipitation relationship in summer or fall (flat constant only, 0.911/0.798), and the paper's own validation explicitly flags EBF (with CRO and OS) as not satisfactorily validated (Fig. 8, off-1:1-line slopes). Uses LAI, not NDVI/EVI, as the vegetation predictor -- not a direct drop-in for a Kc=a*EVI+b form. Genuinely important finding, not just a limitation: EBF is the ONLY land cover in this whole study where Kc does not correlate with the vegetation-index-type variable (LAI) at all -- suggests EBF/tropical forest ET may be fundamentally energy/radiation-limited rather than vegetation-structure-limited, which would mean the whole family of vegetation-index-regression approaches (Kamble, an EVI-adjusted version, this paper's own LAI regression) may be structurally the wrong kind of model for tropical forest Kc, not just missing better-fit coefficients -- worth raising with Becky/Rich directly. One solid usable number regardless: mean annual EBF Kc = 0.75 (+/-0.03) across this study's sites, a real global reference/sanity-check value, though not tropical-specific enough to replace a real regression.
ER  -

